A software gatekeeper for a board whose secure world has no room for one.

WHERE THESE FILES CAME FROM

module.cpp, SoftGateKeeperDevice.cpp, SoftGateKeeperDevice.h and
SoftGateKeeper.h are copied verbatim from AOSP:

    device/generic/goldfish/gatekeeper/

That is the emulator's gatekeeper -- scrypt in the normal world, no secure
hardware behind it. They are kept byte-for-byte so that pulling a newer
upstream version stays a copy rather than a merge; only Android.mk is ours,
and it differs from the emulator's in one line, the module name. The log tag
still says GoldfishGatekeeper on purpose: when something goes wrong it is
worth knowing at a glance which implementation answered.

WHY NOT THE REAL THING

mocha does have a secure world. The stock TOS partition holds NVIDIA's TLK
and it runs: /dev/tlk_device is there, tlk_daemon is up, and the kernel
carries security/tlk_driver. The image bundles seven trusted applications --
oemcrypto_secure_service, crypto_service, secure_otf, secure_rtc,
hdcp_secure_service, hwkeystore and tlkstoragedemo_task -- and none of them
is a gatekeeper. The image is signed by Xiaomi, so an eighth cannot be added,
and replacing the whole trusted OS with OP-TEE is a four-thousand-line
platform port. The kernel tree's docs/trustzone-tlk.md has the full analysis.

So the choice was between this and nothing, and nothing was what we had.

WHAT IT ACTUALLY BUYS

Almost nothing, and that is worth stating plainly. Without a gatekeeper HAL,
gatekeeperd does not fail -- it falls back to the very same code:

    hw_device = IGatekeeper::getService();
    if (hw_device == nullptr) {
        ALOGW("falling back to software GateKeeper");
        soft_device.reset(new SoftGateKeeperDevice());
    }

The lockscreen has therefore always worked, through an identical scrypt
implementation, one process to the left. What changes is that the interface
is now declared and served, so

    E init: Could not find 'android.hardware.gatekeeper@1.0::IGatekeeper/default'

stops appearing, and -- the reason this exists at all -- the device can
satisfy compatibility_matrix.4.xml, which lists gatekeeper 1.0 as
optional="false". Without it, claiming target-level 4 is impossible.

Security is unchanged either way: the signing key in SoftGateKeeper is zeros,
attempt counters live in the normal world, and root can reach them. That is
the honest state of a board whose secure world will not take a passenger.

NOBODY HAS TO RESET THEIR PIN

Adding a HAL where there was none is a case AOSP anticipated. A password
handle enrolled by gatekeeperd's own fallback is marked as not hardware
backed, and verifyChallenge() notices:

    } else {
        // upgrade scenario, a HAL has been added to this device where there was none before
        SoftGateKeeperDevice soft_dev;
        ret = soft_dev.verify(...);
        if (ret == 0) {
            // success! re-enroll with HAL
            if (request_reenroll != nullptr) *request_reenroll = true;
        }
    }

The old handle is checked against a local software device and, once it
passes, the framework is told to re-enroll it through the HAL. The user
enters the PIN they already had, once, and the migration happens underneath.
